<GuideLayout
  title="Ready-to-Ship vs Custom Activewear Production: Which Path Is Better?"
  type="Comparison Center"
  description="Compare ready-to-ship private label activewear and custom OEM production by MOQ, lead time, exclusivity, launch speed and inventory risk."
>
  <p>Ready-to-ship and custom activewear production solve different problems. One helps brands launch faster with lower inventory pressure. The other creates stronger product differentiation and better long-term brand control.</p>

  <h2>Quick Answer</h2>
  <p>Choose ready-to-ship when you need speed, low MOQ and market validation. Choose custom production when your brand needs exclusive fabric, fit, construction, color and product identity.</p>

  <h2>Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Path</th><th>Best Use Case</th><th>Typical Advantage</th><th>Main Limitation</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Ready-to-ship private label</td><td>New brand launch, fitness studio merch, small batch drops</td><td>Fast launch and lower MOQ</td><td>Less fabric and pattern exclusivity</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Logo customization</td><td>Brands that want quick branded products</td><td>Custom brand presence without full development</td><td>Logo approval and placement must be clear</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Custom OEM production</td><td>Established brands building a signature collection</td><td>Control over fit, fabric, color and trims</td><td>Longer sampling and bulk lead time</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How to Decide</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you do not have sales data yet, start with ready-to-ship private label.</li>
    <li>If you have proven demand, move best sellers into custom OEM development.</li>
    <li>If fabric hand feel is your brand moat, plan custom material development early.</li>
    <li>If cash flow is tight, avoid large custom MOQ before testing fit and color demand.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Recommended Launch Route</h2>
  <p>Many activewear brands begin with ready-to-ship leggings, sports bras or yoga sets, then use customer feedback to build an OEM collection. This staged route reduces risk while still allowing long-term product differentiation.</p>

